{"id":"benefit-cost-by-year","title":"The cost of a job beyond pay, year by year","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"What does the City pay for retirement, health and other benefits for each person on the payroll, and how has that moved against salary?","finding":"Benefits cost $1.79 billion in fiscal 2026, 36.7% of regular salary and $43,754 for each person paid; in fiscal 2013 they cost $978.9 million, 40.0% of salary and $26,196 a person. The share fell because the City’s retirement payment did: it peaked at 23.0% of salary in fiscal 2021 and was 15.1% in fiscal 2026. Health and dental cover rose from $9,470 to $17,488 a person (85%), about in step with a rise of 82% in regular salary for each person paid.","how":"City payroll file, fiscal 2013 to 2026: the retirement, health and dental, and other benefits columns, added up for everyone on the file except the Superior Court. The file’s dictionary describes them as “City contributions to employee retirement plan”, “Averaged health and dental benefits to protect employee privacy as legally required.” and, for other benefits, payroll taxes, unemployment insurance and minor benefits. “Per person” divides by the number of names (numbers before fiscal 2017) paid in each department that year, so part-year and part-time staff pull it down. Shares are of regular salary. In this file a full-time year is 2,080 paid hours in every fiscal year from 2017 to 2025 and 2,160 in fiscal 2026 (27 fortnights of 80 hours rather than 26), so every yearly amount for fiscal 2026 is about 3.8% larger than a like-for-like year.","limits":"These are the employer’s costs as the Controller allocates them, not what any employee received: health cover is an average spread across staff, and the retirement figure is the City’s yearly payment into the pension fund, which moves with investment returns and with how fast past shortfalls are paid off, not with what today’s staff will be paid. A falling retirement share therefore says nothing about pensions 